{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"We are asked to find the *second* derivative of a parametric function.\n\nRecall that the *first* derivative of a function defined parametrically by the equations $x=u(t)$ and $y=v(t)$ is found with the following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dt}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}$\n\nThen, the *second* derivative is found with this following rule:\n\n$\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{v'(t)}{u'(t)}\\right)}{u'(t)}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"Let's start by finding $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.\n\n$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}-3}{4}$\n\n[[☃ explanation 1]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"We are given that $x=8\\sqrt{t}+1$ and $y=-6\\sqrt{t}+t$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(-6\\sqrt{t}+t)}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(8\\sqrt{t}+1)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(-6t^{\\frac12}+t)}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(8t^{\\frac12}+1)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{-3t^{-\\frac12}+1}{4t^{-\\frac12}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{-3t^{-\\frac12}}{4t^{-\\frac12}}+\\dfrac{1}{4t^{-\\frac12}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\dfrac{3}{4}+\\dfrac{t^\\frac12}{4}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}-3}{4}\n\\end{align}$","hidePrompt":"Got it, thanks!","showPrompt":"Show entire calculation.","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}},{"content":"Now we can find $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$.\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\right)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{d}{dt}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}-3}{4}\\right)}{\\dfrac{d}{dt}(8\\sqrt{t}+1)}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{t^{-\\frac12}}{8}\\right)}{4t^{-\\frac12}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{1}{32}\n\\end{align}$\n","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\\dfrac{1}{32}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=8\\sqrt{t}+1$ and $y=-6\\sqrt{t}+t$.\n\n**What is $\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ in terms of $t$ ?**\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"radio 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"choices":[{"content":"$\\dfrac{1}{32}$","correct":true},{"content":"$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}-3}{4}$","correct":false},{"content":"$\\dfrac{t-3\\sqrt{t}}{16}$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false},{"content":"$\\dfrac{3t}{2}$","correct":false,"isNoneOfTheAbove":false}],"deselectEnabled":false,"displayCount":null,"hasNoneOfTheAbove":false,"multipleSelect":false,"onePerLine":false,"randomize":true},"static":false,"type":"radio","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
